states: triaging -> planning -> investigating -> synthesizing -> proposing
trajectory: d0929452-2265-4897-8b3f-29095227d0d9
triage judged: investigate (medium confidence, suspects resource_exhaustion)
  Six MongoDB instances alerting simultaneously at the same second points to a shared substrate issue (host, storage, or network) rather than an isolated warning. Too broad and synchronized to dismiss.
investigation: 2 round(s), 7 dispatch(es), 22 step(s)

=== PROPOSAL ===
  action      : restart_service (restart)
  target      : mongodb-user
  expect      : mongod is absent, not sick: SIGTERM at 10:07:18.971, clean teardown, exit 0, no startup line after. Recreating the container should yield fresh mongod startup lines in Loki for the mongodb-user pod ('Waiting for connections' on 27017) and then connection-accepted entries, within ~60s. Caller-side alerts against mongodb-user should clear.
  within      : 300s
  risk        : If the process is being re-killed by an active injector or a controller, the restart is a no-op that costs a pod recreate and masks the real actor. If the ConfigMap-driven admin/failure config is the cause, restarting re-applies it. Any in-flight writes at recreate are lost; mongod state is on disk but in-memory sessions and caches are discarded, and the recreate is not reversible.
  blast radius: One service. Every hotel-reservation caller of mongodb-user (user/auth path) sees connection resets for the recreate duration. Five sibling datastores remain down and unaddressed by this proposal.
  if wrong    : No startup lines appear, or the pod logs another SIGTERM within minutes — meaning an external agent (the suspected failure-injection path) is still stopping these processes and the restart will be undone. Note mongodb-user emits no span metrics [tr_11db3f5b33a4], so confirmation must come from logs and caller alerts, not from its own error ratio.
  rests on    : tr_540a1d20a6c7, tr_11db3f5b33a4
  execution   : not executed - a proposal is a claim, never the change (ADR-0028 §1)

context: 27874 pushed / 7066 pulled (pull rate 0.2022), 2 section(s) dropped

=== VERDICT ===
  fault class : process_freeze
  fix class   : restart
  confidence  : low
  evidence    : tr_e3f5da9a7ba3, tr_540a1d20a6c7, tr_0cd4e97940fd, tr_03f6bbb5b918, tr_044c506c1683
  root cause  : All six mongodb instances in the hotel-reservation namespace were stopped by externally delivered SIGTERMs (kill(2), pid 0/uid 0) at 10:07:17-10:07:18, each completing a clean, fully-sequenced MongoDB shutdown with no errors and no restart afterward [tr_e3f5da9a7ba3][tr_540a1d20a6c7][tr_0cd4e97940fd]. The change log shows platform-automation provisioning these workloads fresh in the ~80 seconds before onset, including an administrative/failure-injection ConfigMap for mongodb-geo [tr_03f6bbb5b918][tr_044c506c1683]. The databases are therefore not failing-but-live: their processes are absent, so callers hit dead endpoints and the alerts at 10:08:17 reflect missing datastores rather than a defect in any one of them. The nearest fit in this taxonomy is a deliberate stop of the running artifacts, remedied by bringing the mongod processes back up.
  OPEN        : Whether the mongod processes stayed down after 10:08:23 or were restarted just outside the queried window - no startup lines were seen, but the logs were truncated.
  OPEN        : Which actor issued the SIGTERMs; pid 0/uid 0 is consistent with kubelet pod termination but the change log does not record a delete or scale-down event.
  OPEN        : Whether the failure-admin-geo ConfigMap drove the termination or is incidental; its contents were never read.
  OPEN        : Whether the other three alerting datastores (profile, rate, recommendation) share the identical signature - they were never dispatched on.

